"""Grade 10 life essentials: love, the body and courage with friends."""
from spec import L, U

N10 = U('Love, the body and courage', 'Morality', 'How do I love well, and how do I stay free when everyone is pushing?', [
L('Dating, desire and respect: what love is for',
  'Teenagers are told that love is a feeling, that it is private, and that the body is just equipment. The Church says something more demanding and more hopeful: that love is the gift of oneself, that the body speaks, and that respect is not the opposite of passion but its protection. This lesson gives a way to think about dating, boundaries and breakups, without shame and without vagueness.',
  '1604-1605,2331-2336,2337-2347,2350,2392-2396,357,2346',
  'Genesis 1:27-28;Song of Songs 8:6-7;1 Corinthians 13:4-7;1 Corinthians 6:19-20',
  'chastity dignity charity virtue',
  'What do the songs, shows and people around you say love is, and what do they leave out?',
  'Write a “respect list” of five things you would want a person who likes you to do, and five you would not tolerate. Then write five things you would promise to do for someone you like. Choose one boundary you will state out loud to yourself and, when needed, to another person.',
  'evening-examen',
  id='10-L1',
  hook='Play or read the chorus of a popular love song. Ask: “What does this say love is? Is that a promise or a mood?” Then read 1 Corinthians 13:4–7 and compare.',
  teach=[
    'The Church starts with a claim about the human person: we are made for love, “in the image of God,” and “God is love” (CCC 1604, 2331). Sexuality is therefore not a separate zone but part of who we are, “affecting all aspects of the human person in the unity of his body and soul” (CCC 2332). The body is not a costume; it is the person, and so what we do with the body says something about what we think of the person.',
    'Chastity is the virtue that integrates sexuality in the person, so that the body can express love truly (CCC 2337). It is not repression: “Chastity means the successful integration of sexuality within the person” and so “self-mastery... is a training in human freedom” (CCC 2339–2341). This is why the Catechism calls it a long project, “never acquired once and for all,” with growth through friendship and grace (CCC 2342–2347).',
    'The key measure of any relationship is whether it treats the other person as a gift or as a means. “Love is the fundamental and innate vocation of every human being” (CCC 2392). Karol Wojtyla, later John Paul II, put it simply: the opposite of love is not hate but use. The Catechism says plainly that sexual acts are made for the total gift of marriage, which is why the Church asks for patience before it (CCC 2360–2362, 2348–2350). “Those who are engaged to marry are called to live chastity in continence” (CCC 2350).',
    'Practically, respect shows in small things: asking, listening, saying no without shame, not pressuring, not sharing private images, being the same in public and private. Good dating is a friendship that is going somewhere, with others around you, and it is allowed to end kindly. Breakups are moral too: do not betray private things you were told, and do not spread stories to get even (see CCC 2477, 2489).',
  ],
  talk=[
    'What would “being used” feel like? What would “being loved” feel like?',
    'What is one boundary you think is wise, and how would you say it?',
    'What does it mean to be the same person in public and in private?',
    'How should someone end a relationship respectfully?',
  ],
  quiz=[
    'What does the Church say sexuality is?|A purely private matter|Part of the whole person, affecting body and soul|Only a biological function|Something shameful|1|CCC 2332.',
    'What is chastity?|Repressing desire|The integration of sexuality within the person, so the body can express love truly|Never dating|Just a rule|1|CCC 2337.',
    'What does the Church say about engaged couples?|They may do as they like|They are called to live chastity in continence|They should avoid each other|Nothing|1|CCC 2350.',
  ],
  life='Pick one boundary and one habit for the next month. Boundary: where, when and with whom you spend time. Habit: one way you will actively treat someone with respect, like a text that says you value them, or a plan with a group. Tell a trusted friend or adult so someone can ask you.',
  saint='Karol Wojtyla (John Paul II): as a young priest he walked and kayaked with groups of university students and young couples, listening to their questions about love, and wrote Love and Responsibility from what he heard.',
  misc=[
    '“The Church thinks sex is dirty.”',
    '“If we love each other, it is fine.”',
    '“Chastity is only for people who cannot find a date.”',
  ],
  deeper=['apol:chastity', 'apol:marriage-divorce'],
  mem='“Do you not know that your body is a temple of the Holy Spirit?” (1 Corinthians 6:19)',
  assess='Ask each student to explain in their own words what chastity is and is not, and to state one boundary and one respectful habit.',
  script=dict(
    open='Read the chorus of a current popular love song aloud (without playing it). Ask, “What does it say love is? Is that a promise or a feeling?” Take three answers. Say, “Let us see what the Church has to add.”',
    teach=[
      'Say: “The Church begins with this: you are made for love. Not just romance; love as the gift of yourself. And the body is part of you, not a costume. So what you do with your body says something about what you think of the person. That is why this matters.”',
      'Say: “Chastity is a word people mishear. The Catechism, 2337, says it is the integration of sexuality within the person, so that the body can express love truly. It is a form of freedom. A chaste person is not someone with no desires; it is someone who is not run by them. Number 2342 says it is never acquired once and for all. It is a lifelong project, and grace helps.”',
      'Say: “Here is a test for any relationship: does this treat the other person as a gift or as a means? Karol Wojtyla, who became John Paul II, said the opposite of love is not hate, it is use. You can feel strongly for someone and still use them. The Church asks for patience before the full gift of marriage because the body’s language is total, and it deserves a total promise.”',
      'Say: “In practice, respect looks small. Asking. Listening. Not pressuring. Not sharing private photos. Being the same person in public and in private. And ending a relationship kindly. You can be a person people feel safe with. That is a rare and good thing.”',
    ],
    ask=[
      'What does it feel like to be treated as a gift? As a means?',
      'What is one boundary you think is wise? How would you say it kindly?',
      'What makes it hard to hold a boundary, and who can help you?',
    ],
    practice=[
      'Give each student a card. Write five things you would want from someone who likes you, and five you would not tolerate.',
      'Write five promises you would make to someone you like.',
      'Pick one boundary and say it aloud once, quietly, to yourself, to practice.',
    ],
    close='Pray the evening examen briefly. Say, “You are worth being treated with respect. Treat others the same way.”'),
  tryit=['Write five things you want and five you will not tolerate.', 'Write five promises you would make.', 'Say one boundary out loud to practice.'],
  saintStory='Karol Wojtyla was born in Wadowice, Poland, in 1920. By twenty-one he had lost his mother, his brother and his father. During the Second World War he worked in a quarry and a chemical factory to avoid deportation, and secretly studied for the priesthood. He was ordained in 1946.\nAs a young priest in Kraków, he spent a lot of time with university students. He organized hiking and kayaking trips with them, where they talked and prayed in the open air, and he called the group “Środowisko,” the environment. He listened closely to their questions about love, dating and marriage, and later said that these conversations shaped his thinking. He wrote Love and Responsibility in 1960 on the basis of what he heard.\nIn 1978 he became Pope John Paul II, and led the Church for more than 26 years. He started World Youth Day to speak directly to young people. He died on April 2, 2005, and was canonized in 2014.',
),

L('Pornography, shame and getting free',
  'Many young people meet pornography early, and many carry secrecy and shame about it. The Church’s teaching is clear that it harms the viewer and the people on the screen, and it is just as clear that the answer is mercy, practical help, and a way forward that works. This lesson is not about blame. It is about freedom, grace, honest friends and small, repeatable steps.',
  '2354,2351-2353,2339-2345,2520-2527,1420-1424,1451,1496',
  'John 8:1-11;Romans 7:15-25;Psalm 51;Luke 15:11-24',
  'chastity sin mercy reconciliation grace',
  'What does shame do to a person, and what does it make us hide?',
  'Write a private “plan for freedom” in three parts: (1) practical changes (filters, phone out of the bedroom at night, moving off a certain site), (2) people (one trusted person to tell), and (3) spirit (a short daily prayer and a plan for Confession). Keep it on a card, and do the first item tonight.',
  'contrition',
  id='10-L2',
  hook='Read the first half of the story of the prodigal son (Luke 15:11–20), stopping at “while he was yet at a distance, his father saw him.” Ask: “Where was the father looking? What did he do before the son said a word?”',
  teach=[
    'Be plain, and be kind. The Catechism says pornography “offends against chastity because it perverts the conjugal act” and it “does grave injury to the dignity of its participants (actors, vendors, the public), since each one becomes an object of base pleasure and illicit profit for others” (CCC 2354). Notice who is harmed: the viewer, and also real people on the screen, who are often exploited. That is why the Church calls it a grave matter.',
    'Compulsion is also real. The Catechism notes that the imputability and responsibility for acts can be lessened by “immaturity, force of acquired habit, inordinate fears, anxiety, or other psychological or social factors” (CCC 2352, 1735). That is not an excuse; it is mercy. Many struggle for years, and the answer is not despair. “Chastity... is a long and exacting work” (CCC 2342).',
    'Shame says hide; grace says come out. The story of the Prodigal Son shows a father who runs to the son before he finishes his speech (Luke 15:20). Jesus says to the woman in John 8, “Neither do I condemn you; go, and do not sin again.” The sacrament of Penance is where mercy is real and concrete: it is both forgiveness and strength, and priests have heard everything (CCC 1422–1424, 1496). Go, and go again.',
    'Practice matters. The Catechism names the means: “prayer, the sacraments, avoiding occasions of sin, and self-denial” (CCC 2340–2345), and “friendship” (CCC 2347). In practice: devices out of the bedroom at night, filters and accountability apps, telling a trusted adult or friend, filling empty time with sleep, exercise, and real friends. If you are stuck, talk to a counselor. You deserve help.',
  ],
  talk=[
    'Why does shame make things worse?',
    'Who are the people hurt by pornography besides the viewer?',
    'What does it mean that the father ran to the son?',
    'What is one practical change you could make tonight?',
  ],
  quiz=[
    'What does the Catechism say about pornography?|It is harmless|It does grave injury to the dignity of those involved and offends against chastity|It is a private matter|It is allowed in moderation|1|CCC 2354.',
    'Does the Church say habit and psychological factors can lessen responsibility?|Never|Yes, they can lessen responsibility but not remove the need for help and growth|They remove all responsibility|Only for adults|1|CCC 2352 and 1735.',
    'What is the Church’s answer to shame?|Hide it|Confession, mercy, friends and practical steps|Ignore it|Punishment|1|CCC 1422–1424; Luke 15.',
  ],
  life='Take one practical step tonight (a phone in another room at night, a filter, deleting an app). Tell one trusted person that you are working on it, or if that is too hard, write it on a card you will bring to Confession. Schedule Confession this month.',
  saint='Augustine of Hippo: he prayed, “Give me chastity and continence, but not yet,” and was set free through grace, friends and a mother’s tears.',
  misc=[
    '“Everyone does it, so it does no harm.”',
    '“If I slip, I might as well give up.”',
    '“God is disgusted with me.”',
  ],
  deeper=['apol:chastity', 'apol:confession'],
  mem='“Create in me a clean heart, O God, and put a new and right spirit within me.” (Psalm 51:10)',
  assess='Ask each student to name two harms of pornography, explain why shame is not the answer, and state their first step for freedom.',
  script=dict(
    open='Say, in a calm voice, “Today is a topic that many people carry in secret. I am not going to ask anyone to share anything private. The goal is freedom, not shame.” Read Luke 15:20: “While he was still far off, his father saw him and ran.” Ask, “What did the father do before the son spoke?”',
    teach=[
      'Say: “First, honesty. The Church says pornography harms. The Catechism, 2354, says it offends against chastity and does grave injury to the dignity of everyone involved. Real people are used, often exploited. And it trains the viewer to see people as objects. That is why the Church takes it seriously.”',
      'Say: “Second, mercy. Many people struggle with this, especially young ones. The Catechism says habit, anxiety and other factors can lessen responsibility (2352, 1735). That is not an excuse; it is a reason to get help, not despair. Chastity is a long project and never done once and for all (2342). A fall is not the end. The father runs to the son.”',
      'Say: “Third, shame. Shame says hide. Grace says come out. In John 8, Jesus says, ‘Neither do I condemn you; go, and do not sin again.’ Both halves matter. The sacrament of Penance is where this becomes real: forgiveness and strength. Priests have heard everything. You will not shock them. Go, and go again.”',
      'Say: “Fourth, practice. The Catechism names the means: prayer, the sacraments, avoiding the occasions, self-denial, and friendship. In life: devices out of the bedroom at night, filters, accountability, telling someone you trust, sleep, exercise, real friends. If you are stuck, a counselor is not a failure of faith. It is wisdom.”',
    ],
    ask=[
      'What do you think shame does to a person?',
      'Why is telling one trusted person so powerful?',
      'What is one practical change you could make tonight?',
    ],
    practice=[
      'Hand out private cards. Write the three parts of a plan: practical, people, spirit.',
      'Do not share. Pick the first practical step and write the time you will do it tonight.',
      'Write when you will go to Confession. Put the card in your wallet.',
    ],
    close='Pray the act of contrition together slowly. Say, “You are not your worst moment. Take one step toward freedom, and take it with someone.”'),
  tryit=['Write a three-part plan: practical, people, spirit.', 'Do the first step tonight.', 'Pick a date for Confession.'],
  saintStory='Augustine was born in 354 in Thagaste, in North Africa. His mother Monica was a Christian, and his father a pagan. A brilliant student, he went to Carthage at sixteen and fell into a life he later described with candor: he took a concubine, had a son, Adeodatus, and chased fame as a teacher of rhetoric. He followed the Manichees for nine years.\nMonica prayed for him for years, and followed him to Italy. In Milan, he listened to Bishop Ambrose preach, and began to see the Christian faith as intellectually serious. But he could not change his life. He famously prayed, “Give me chastity and continence, but not yet.”\nIn the summer of 386, in a garden in Milan, he heard a child’s voice say, “Take and read.” He opened a book of Paul’s letters at Romans 13:13–14: “Not in orgies and drunkenness... but put on the Lord Jesus Christ.” He was baptized by Ambrose at Easter 387. He became bishop of Hippo and wrote the Confessions, a book about God’s patient mercy. He died in 430.',
),

L('Courage with friends: peer pressure and standing alone',
  'Peer pressure is not just a teenage problem, but at sixteen it is loud. The Church’s answer is more interesting than “just say no.” It takes pressure seriously (it can reduce blame), it takes courage seriously (it is a virtue you can train), and it takes friendship seriously (the right people make courage easier). The lesson teaches students to choose their company, rehearse their lines, and carry a quiet witness.',
  '1735,1808,1816,2471-2474,2284-2287,1802,1939',
  'Daniel 3:13-30;Matthew 10:32-33;Proverbs 13:20;2 Timothy 1:7-8',
  'fortitude virtue conscience martyr',
  'When did you last go along with something you did not want to do? What made it feel impossible to say no?',
  'Write three situations where you might face pressure (a party, a group chat, a test, a joke). For each, write one line you could say, one exit, and one person you could text. Practice saying the lines aloud to a partner.',
  'st-francis',
  id='10-L3',
  hook='Ask students to stand in a line from “easy to say no” to “impossible to say no” based on an imagined scenario (a friend offers a drink, a friend wants to copy homework, a friend makes a cruel joke). Ask: “Why is the line different each time?”',
  teach=[
    'Be honest about pressure. The Catechism says that responsibility for an act can be “diminished or even nullified” by “ignorance, inadvertence, duress, fear, habit, inordinate attachments, and other psychological or social factors” (CCC 1735). That is a mercy, and it is also a map: pressure is real, but it does not take away your freedom, and your freedom grows when you practise it.',
    'Courage is a virtue, not a personality. Fortitude “ensures firmness in difficulties and constancy in the pursuit of the good” and it “strengthens the resolve to resist temptations and to overcome obstacles in the moral life” (CCC 1808). The Catechism lists as its highest form the readiness to “confess the faith” without shame (CCC 1816). It is trained by small acts: saying no on a small thing makes the large one possible.',
    'Friends matter more than willpower. “Whoever walks with the wise becomes wise, but the companion of fools will suffer harm” (Proverbs 13:20). The Catechism speaks of the duty to give witness, and of those who lead others to sin by pressure as bearing a share of the responsibility (CCC 2284–2287, 2471–2473). So a friend group is a moral environment. Choose it. And be the kind of friend who makes “no” easier for others.',
    'Think of the three young men in Daniel 3 who refused to bow to the king’s statue: “If our God is able to save us... But if not, be it known to you, O king, that we will not serve your gods.” They did not know it would turn out well; they just knew what they would not do. The witness of a Christian is often quiet and unglamorous, a steady no, without contempt for others.',
  ],
  talk=[
    'Which situations make it hardest for you to say no? Why?',
    'What is the difference between standing alone and being proud?',
    'What kind of friends help you be your best self?',
    'How can you be the person who makes it easier for someone else to say no?',
  ],
  quiz=[
    'What does the Catechism say about pressure and responsibility?|Pressure removes all responsibility|Pressure can lessen responsibility but does not remove freedom|Pressure does not matter|Only adults feel it|1|CCC 1735.',
    'What is fortitude?|Never being afraid|Firmness in difficulties and constancy in pursuing the good|Being stubborn|Strength of body|1|CCC 1808.',
    'Why does choosing friends matter?|It does not|Companions shape us, and good friends make courage easier|Only popularity matters|Friends should all agree|1|Proverbs 13:20; CCC 2284–2287.',
  ],
  life='Pick one setting this month where pressure is real. Decide your line, your exit, and your helper. Practice the line aloud. Also do one thing to make no easier for someone else: invite a friend to something good, or back someone up when they say no.',
  saint='Pier Giorgio Frassati: a lively Italian student who loved mountains, jokes and the poor, and who stood up to his friends and family for his faith.',
  misc=[
    '“If I am under pressure, I am not responsible.”',
    '“Courage means never being afraid.”',
    '“Refusing makes me better than other people.”',
  ],
  deeper=['room:aug-confessions'],
  mem='“God did not give us a spirit of timidity but a spirit of power and love and self-control.” (2 Timothy 1:7)',
  assess='Ask each student to define fortitude, explain how pressure affects responsibility, and show a line, an exit and a helper.',
  script=dict(
    open='Mark a line on the floor from “easy to say no” to “impossible to say no.” Read three scenarios and ask students to stand where they would be. Say, “Notice that the line is different every time, and that it is okay.”',
    teach=[
      'Say: “Pressure is real. The Catechism says responsibility can be reduced by fear, duress, habit and social factors (1735). That is a mercy. But it is also a map. It shows you the places where it will be hard. So you can prepare.”',
      'Say: “Courage is a virtue, and virtues grow with practice. Fortitude, says number 1808, is firmness in difficulties and constancy in pursuing the good. You do not become brave by waiting for a big moment. You become brave by saying no on small things, so that when the big one comes you have practised.”',
      'Say: “Your friends matter more than your willpower. Proverbs says the companion of fools suffers harm. Choose your company with care. And be that friend to others. When one person says no, it is easier for the next one. Everyone is waiting for someone else to go first.”',
      'Say: “Look at Daniel 3. Three young men refuse to bow to a golden statue. They say, ‘God can save us. But if not, we still will not.’ They do not know how it will end. They know what they will not do. That is what a witness looks like: steady, not loud, and without contempt for others.”',
    ],
    ask=[
      'What are the two or three situations where you most feel pressure?',
      'What is one line you could actually say? Try it aloud.',
      'Who is the friend who makes it easier for you to be your best?',
    ],
    practice=[
      'Give each student a card with three columns: Line, Exit, Helper.',
      'Fill in three situations and practise the lines with a partner.',
      'Write the name of one friend you will back up this month.',
    ],
    close='Pray the prayer of St. Francis together. Say, “Courage is a habit. Start small, and start now.”'),
  tryit=['Write a line, an exit and a helper for three situations.', 'Practise the lines aloud.', 'Name one friend you will back up.'],
  saintStory='Pier Giorgio Frassati was born in Turin in 1901 to a wealthy, non-practicing family; his father owned a major newspaper. He was not a pious-looking teenager: he loved practical jokes, mountain climbing, skiing and cards, and he had a big group of friends he called the “Tipi Loschi,” the shady characters.\nBut he went to Mass every day, spent hours in adoration, and gave his time and pocket money to the poor, often secretly. He once gave away his coat in the cold, and sometimes was found riding the third-class train so that he could give away the difference in ticket cost. His friends said he made faith look strong and cheerful. He was active in politics, speaking out against Mussolini’s regime, and once defended a Catholic procession against attackers.\nIn 1925, at 24, he caught polio, probably from the sick he visited. He died on July 4. On his deathbed he was still worried about a poor family he had been helping, and wrote a note with the address. Thousands of poor people came to his funeral, which surprised his family. He was beatified in 1990 and canonized in 2025. He said, “Live, don’t just exist.”',
),
])
